WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Sub-agent design is where most multi-agent systems break down in practice.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.119
For the next few minutes, we'll cover how to scope authority correctly,

3
00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:15.119
manage context isolation, and write instructions that produce consistent and predictable output.

4
00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:20.959
We'll also cover the most common anti-patterns that make sub-agents brittle and hard to debug.

5
00:00:20.959 --> 00:00:21.840
Let's get into it.

6
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.159
Spawn a sub-agent and it wakes up knowing nothing.

7
00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:31.120
Not your conversation, not the master plan, not what the other sub-agents are doing.

8
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.319
That's context isolation, and it's the single most misunderstood property

9
00:00:36.319 --> 00:00:38.880
of the orchestrator's sub-agent model.

10
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.080
A sub-agent is a separate agent instance the orchestrator creates to handle

11
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.080
one delegated sub-task, with its own context window and its own tool permissions.

12
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.680
What it can do is bounded by an authority boundary,

13
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.959
the explicit set of actions it's permitted to take.

14
00:00:56.959 --> 00:00:59.439
The principle there is least privilege.

15
00:00:59.439 --> 00:01:04.639
A sub-agent should be able to do exactly what its task requires and nothing beyond it.

16
00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:11.120
Isolation and least privilege together are what keeps sub-agents predictable and testable.

17
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.319
Context isolation is often stated but rarely fully understood.

18
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:22.559
When the orchestrator spawns a sub-agent, the sub-agent starts with a blank context window.

19
00:01:22.559 --> 00:01:25.760
It doesn't inherit the orchestrator's conversation history.

20
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.239
It doesn't see what other sub-agents are doing.

21
00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:31.919
It doesn't have access to the system prompt or the master plan

22
00:01:31.919 --> 00:01:37.599
unless the orchestrator explicitly copies that information into the sub-agent's instructions.

23
00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:40.559
This isn't a bug or a limitation of the architecture.

24
00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:43.199
It's how the system is designed to work.

25
00:01:43.199 --> 00:01:45.919
Every piece of context the sub-agent needs

26
00:01:45.919 --> 00:01:51.040
has to be passed explicitly by the orchestrator at the time of task assignment.

27
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.239
If something isn't passed, the sub-agent simply doesn't have it.

28
00:01:55.519 --> 00:01:59.680
Context isolation benefits the system in three concrete ways.

29
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.319
First, it prevents context bloat.

30
00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:06.480
Sub-agents don't accumulate irrelevant history from other parts of the pipeline,

31
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.559
which keeps each sub-agent's context window focused and minimal.

32
00:02:10.559 --> 00:02:12.960
Second, it limits information leakage.

33
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.600
If one sub-task involves sensitive data,

34
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.279
isolation prevents that data from bleeding into unrelated sub-tasks.

35
00:02:19.919 --> 00:02:22.320
Third, it reduces token cost.

36
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.880
Every token in a sub-agent's context window costs money and latency.

37
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.399
Keeping that window minimal is a real optimization.

38
00:02:30.399 --> 00:02:31.839
There's also a testing benefit.

39
00:02:31.839 --> 00:02:35.199
Because sub-agents don't depend on hidden orchestrator state,

40
00:02:35.199 --> 00:02:37.199
they can be tested in isolation.

41
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:40.240
You can validate a sub-agent's behavior independently

42
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.559
before integrating it into a live pipeline.

43
00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:46.479
When designing what context to pass to a sub-agent,

44
00:02:46.479 --> 00:02:48.080
the guiding question is,

45
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.279
what does this specific sub-task actually require?

46
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.399
Pass the task-specific instructions,

47
00:02:54.399 --> 00:02:57.520
the data or content needed to complete the work,

48
00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.520
the required output format,

49
00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.880
and any constraints the sub-agent has to respect.

50
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.880
Withhold everything else by default.

51
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.160
That includes the orchestrator's full conversation history,

52
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.240
outputs from other sub-agents,

53
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.119
system prompts that aren't relevant to this task,

54
00:03:13.119 --> 00:03:16.479
and credentials or permissions that aren't needed here.

55
00:03:16.479 --> 00:03:19.119
The default should always be minimum necessary.

56
00:03:19.119 --> 00:03:22.080
You can add context if the sub-agent needs it.

57
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.000
But starting broad and trimming down is harder and riskier

58
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.199
than starting minimal and adding deliberately.

59
00:03:30.399 --> 00:03:34.320
Authority boundaries define what a sub-agent is allowed to do.

60
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.559
The design principle is least privilege.

61
00:03:36.559 --> 00:03:41.839
Grant access only to the tools and data the sub-task actually requires.

62
00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:46.000
If a sub-agent is summarizing text, it doesn't need file write permissions.

63
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.479
If it's querying a database, it doesn't need access to external APIs.

64
00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:54.720
Stating explicit boundaries in the sub-agent's instructions also matters.

65
00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.679
Telling the sub-agent what's in scope and what's out of scope

66
00:03:57.679 --> 00:04:01.520
reduces the chance it makes a decision outside its intended role.

67
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.880
And sub-agents should avoid side effects by default.

68
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.000
If a sub-agent modifies shared state or takes actions outside its assigned scope,

69
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.520
those changes propagate in ways the orchestrator may not be tracking.

70
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:20.959
The quality of sub-agent instructions directly determines how predictable the output will be. Three elements.

71
00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:26.000
Every sub-agent instruction set needs a narrow scope definition

72
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.239
that tells the sub-agent exactly what the task is and what it isn't.

73
00:04:30.239 --> 00:04:32.880
A precise output format specification,

74
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.239
so the orchestrator can reliably aggregate what comes back,

75
00:04:36.239 --> 00:04:38.480
and explicit success criteria,

76
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.359
so the sub-agent knows when the task is complete.

77
00:04:41.679 --> 00:04:44.959
When any of these three elements is missing or vague,

78
00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:48.480
output quality drops and becomes inconsistent.

79
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.920
Inconsistent sub-agent output is among the hardest things to debug in a multi-agent pipeline

80
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.399
because the failures look different each run and the root cause isn't obvious.

81
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.320
A well-designed sub-agent is independently testable and substitutable.

82
00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.839
Independently testable means you can run the sub-agent with only its explicit inputs

83
00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:15.279
and validate its output without needing the orchestrator or any other sub-agent in the loop.

84
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.359
Substitutable means you can swap one implementation of a sub-agent for another

85
00:05:21.359 --> 00:05:23.600
without changing the rest of the system,

86
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.440
as long as the input and output contracts stay the same.

87
00:05:28.079 --> 00:05:31.119
Both properties depend on the same design requirement.

88
00:05:31.119 --> 00:05:32.799
No hidden dependencies.

89
00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:38.559
If a sub-agent silently relies on something the orchestrator doesn't explicitly pass,

90
00:05:38.559 --> 00:05:42.399
it can't be tested alone and it can't be swapped out cleanly.

91
00:05:42.399 --> 00:05:45.359
If you can't test a sub-agent in isolation,

92
00:05:45.359 --> 00:05:50.079
that's a strong signal it has too broad a scope or too many implicit dependencies.

93
00:05:51.359 --> 00:05:56.160
Three anti-patterns show up repeatedly in sub-agent design failures.

94
00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.880
Overly broad scope is the most common.

95
00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.679
The sub-agent is asked to handle too much at once,

96
00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:05.760
which makes instructions ambiguous and outputs harder to validate.

97
00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.160
Underpowered context is the second.

98
00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.839
The orchestrator didn't pass enough information for the sub-agent to complete the task correctly,

99
00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:18.000
so it makes assumptions that may not align with the orchestrator's intent.

100
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.839
Unclear success criteria is the third.

101
00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:25.600
The sub-agent doesn't know definitively when the task is done,

102
00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.320
leading to incomplete outputs or outputs that need significant post-processing.

103
00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.320
Each of these anti-patterns produces a different failure mode,

104
00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.000
but all three are preventable with deliberate instruction design

105
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.359
and explicit context passing at the orchestrator level.

106
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.880
What a sub-agent is given and what it can reach for are two separate things.

107
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.480
Nothing is inherited, so anything you don't hand over simply isn't there for the sub-agent.

108
00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:56.559
Treat that as the design rather than a gap,

109
00:06:56.559 --> 00:06:59.519
and least privilege stops feeling like a restriction.

110
00:07:00.239 --> 00:07:04.720
A sub-agent you can test on its own is a sub-agent you can swap out later.

111
00:07:05.679 --> 00:07:08.720
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

